# a-trench-you-fall-into — BACKLOG C10, `@D006` closed, and it is
# BACKLOG C5's own headline arriving two phases late.
#
# ⚠⚠ **C5 was designed around *the depth is the COST, because the crew
# and the player HOVER and fall in*, and a probe falsified it before a
# line shipped** (`@X282`): `walk_vehicle` was read by NOTHING, so the
# player was stopped by flat sea exactly as a robot is and a 3.0 m boost
# did not cross a 1 m ditch.
#
# ⚠ **The probe was right about the CODE and the design was right about
# the GAME.**  With the column read, this file is that design:
#
#   1. the vehicle drives east and **falls INTO the trench** — a drop is
#      free, so it enters the hex it used to stop one short of;
#   2. it is **still there twenty ticks later**, because climbing the
#      metre back out wants more than `hover_clearance_idle` (0.4 m) has;
#   3. **boost is the way out** — 3.0 m against one metre — which makes
#      `docs/PLAYING.md`'s *the only way out of a base you have sealed*
#      true of trenches again, as it always was of walls.
#
# ⚠ **And the crew cannot do (3).**  They share the chassis and the
# hover, and they have no boost — so a helper that drives into a trench
# is in it for the run.  That is the cost `a-base-behind-a-moat.keys`
# records from the other side, and here it is the same rule producing it
# rather than a separate refusal.

echo a trench you fall into: the depth is the cost, and boost is the way out

do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target
count markers 1 1
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# A one-hex trench right across the lane, a metre below the bank.
palette 1
drag 5 -2 5 2
kind 5 0 water
count painted 80 80

# ── 1. It falls IN ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ (5, 0), not (4, 0): before C10 the vehicle stopped one hex SHORT of
# the water it could not enter.  Now it enters and the trench keeps it.
park 0 0
drive 11 0
tick 6
vehicle 5 0

# ── 2. And it stays ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ Twenty more ticks — sixty hexes of allowance — pointed at the far
# side, and it has not moved: the climb OUT is a metre and a cruising
# hover has 0.4 m.
tick 20
vehicle 5 0

# ── 3. Boost is the way out ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ 3.0 m against the metre the hex sits below its bank, and it does not
# merely leave the trench — it finishes the drive it was on.
boost
tick 4
vehicle 11 0

# ── ⚠ And the crew have no boost ────────────────────────────────
#
# The same chassis, the same hover, one thing missing.  A helper sent
# across is in the trench for the run — which is what
# `a-base-behind-a-moat.keys` records as *the crew dug themselves in*,
# produced here by the rule rather than by a separate refusal.
crew 0 1
send 0 11 1
tick 8
helper 0 5 1
tick 20
helper 0 5 1

snap c10_a_trench_you_fall_into
